  "notes": "⛔ **The method is a stated formula and the model is that formula: take P-Funk, slow it down, add live bass and \"those worm sounds\".** So the tempo band is the low end of the lane, the harmony inherits the source's flat-7-heavy funk vocabulary rather than a minor pop cycle, and the layering is dense — \"3–5 parts\" that still read \"darkly melodic\" is the highest `countermelody.densityRatio` of the producer group and a `melody.densityPerBar` reaching 8. Live bass is the core ingredient, \"deep, melodic, slides between roots, never kick-locked\", which is the lowest `followRootsProb` in this batch at 0.28 with a 22-semitone register. The whining Moog worm lead is the genre's own `timbreHint` and is inherited — this entry is where it comes from. 4- and 8-bar cycles are why `melody.phraseBars` weights 4 hardest. `high` is the research's own rating. ⚠ **The two documented transition devices are authored as percussion lanes, which is the only place the engine has for them**: `reverse` is the backward-masked drum the research names as a fill device, and `impact` is the \"jarring synth hit\". They are sprinkled at a perc density rather than placed at section boundaries, because `percs` has no section-aware placement — the request is honest, its timing is approximate. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 48 to 44 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 45 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 4.",
